<h2>The statutory chain that ends in the balance sheet</h2>
<p>The MSMED Act&rsquo;s payment discipline begins at Section 15: a buyer must pay a micro or small supplier on or before the agreed date — which cannot exceed forty-five days from the day of acceptance or deemed acceptance — and, absent an agreement, before the appointed day. Section 16 prices the default: compound interest, with monthly rests, at three times the bank rate notified by the Reserve Bank. Section 17 confirms the buyer&rsquo;s liability for the amount with that interest. Sections 22 and 23 then make the default impossible to hide and expensive to carry.</p>

<h2>Section 22: the compulsory confession</h2>
<p>For any buyer whose annual accounts are audited, Section 22 mandates disclosure of the full anatomy of its MSME indebtedness:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Unpaid amounts.</strong> The principal and the interest due to micro and small suppliers remaining unpaid at the end of the accounting year, stated separately.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Belated payments.</strong> Interest paid under Section 16 along with amounts paid beyond the appointed day during the year.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Delay interest.</strong> Interest due and payable for the period of delay on payments made during the year without the Section 16 interest.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The running meter.</strong> Interest accrued and remaining unpaid, and the further interest due in succeeding years until the dues are actually paid.</div>
</div>
<p>Because the disclosure sits in audited statements, it engages the statutory auditor&rsquo;s duties and the company-law consequences of misstatement. Suppliers&rsquo; counsel should routinely pull a defaulting buyer&rsquo;s filed financial statements: an admitted MSME-dues line item is close to conclusive corroboration before a Facilitation Council, while a conspicuous absence invites uncomfortable questions the buyer must answer somewhere.</p>

<h2>Section 23: interest with no tax shield</h2>
<p>Ordinarily, interest paid on trade dues is deductible business expenditure. Section 23 reverses that for MSMED interest: notwithstanding anything in the Income-tax Act, 1961, the interest payable or paid under the MSMED Act shall not be allowed as a deduction. The buyer pays the supplier compound interest at a penal rate and then pays tax as if that outflow never happened. Combined with Section 43B(h) of the Income-tax Act — which, from assessment year 2024-25, defers deduction of the principal itself until payment within Section 15 timelines — the fiscal system now pushes in the same direction as the Facilitation Council.</p>

<h2>Using the provisions in practice</h2>
<p>A well-drafted demand notice to a corporate buyer does more than cite Sections 15 to 17; it reminds the buyer of what Sections 22 and 23 are doing to its accounts while the dispute pends — a growing, disclosed, non-deductible liability that auditors must carry forward year after year. For buyers, the counsel is symmetrical: dispute genuine quality claims on the record promptly, pay undisputed portions within the statutory window, and ensure the Section 22 disclosure is accurate, because an understated liability compounds legal exposure rather than deferring it.</p>
<div class="note">Sections 22 and 23 apply to dues of micro and small enterprises; medium enterprises stand outside the delayed-payment chapter&rsquo;s core protections. Supplier status is anchored in Udyam registration and the memorandum filed under Section 8, so both sides should verify registration dates against the supply timeline — a recurring battleground in Council proceedings. This article explains the statute generally and is not advice on any specific matter.</div>
